  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1: Unseasonably mild through Friday with 2-week dry\nspell coming to an end \n\nAnomalous upper level ridging and south-southwest flow aloft\nwill maintain unseasonably warm temperatures through late week.\nMax/Min temps will be 10 to 20 degrees above the historical \naverage for the first week of November, but should fall short of\ndaily records. \n\nA vertically stacked and weakening surface low pressure system\nwill send a front through our area Wednesday night into \nThursday morning. A band of rain showers will accompany this \nsurface feature with accumulations looking to remain under half\nan inch. This cold frontal feature looks to stall near the \ncoast Thursday as it becomes more strung out. \n\nAn approaching shortwave through though will likely reinvigorate\nprecipitation development along the frontal system. The main \narea of uncertainty is if this new area of precipitation will \nimpact much of our area. Global models have trended towards \nkeeping the bulk of this precipitation closer to the coast, but \nsome showers may still nudge into our southeastern area Thursday\nnight into Friday. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2: Trending cooler this weekend with some showers \ndownwind of Lake Erie\n\nA compact shortwave moving through from the Great Lakes will \ninduce a pattern of cooler cyclonic WNW flow pattern this\nweekend. This favors a pattern of lake effect showers off of\nLake Erie moving southeastward and cooler overall temperatures. \nLatest model data favors this activity mostly on Saturday.",
